# The programs that decide whether a pull request is safe.
#
# These are NOT application dependencies. They are the scanners and the test runner the merge
# gate runs, and swapping one for a poisoned build is the highest-leverage supply-chain move
# against this repository. It was also, until this file existed, the one dependency change
# nothing asked a human about: `backend/requirements*` sat in every "needs a human" list while
# the programs judging the pull request were installed unpinned, straight from the index.
#
# This is the INPUT. requirements-ci.txt is generated from it with every transitive dependency
# pinned and hashed (ADR-0018 rule 3). Until 2026-09-25 only these four top-level tools were
# pinned, so everything they pulled in — semgrep alone brings dozens of packages — was whatever
# PyPI served that morning, unverified. Regenerate after editing this file:
#
#   uv pip compile requirements-ci.in --universal --python-version 3.12 --generate-hashes \
#     -o requirements-ci.txt
#
# scripts/check_pins.py reconciles this file with the lock in BOTH directions, and CI installs
# with --require-hashes, so a lock that was not regenerated fails the build instead of
# installing something unreviewed.
#
# CI uses the lock as a CONSTRAINTS file (`pip install --require-hashes -c requirements-ci.txt
# <tool>`): one source of truth for every version and hash, while each job installs only the
# tool it runs. The static job first proves pip enforces the hashes that way, with a canary.
#
# Both files are matched by `GUARD_FILE_RE` in scripts/meta_guard.py: changing either needs the
# owner's `guardrail-change` label. Bumps arrive as Dependabot PRs (pip ecosystem, "/"), after
# the 7-day cooldown that the sensitive-path waiver in ci.yml assumes.
pre-commit==4.6.2
semgrep==1.179.0
pytest==9.1.1
pyyaml==6.0.3
# Security floor for the CI tools' JWT dependency; explicit owner-reviewed pin.
pyjwt==2.15.0
